 "chunks": [
  {
   "text": "Here's the point.",
   "start": 0.725,
   "end": 1.887
  },
  {
   "text": "Each wave's size is x to the power of its zero's real part.",
   "start": 1.887,
   "end": 5.888
  },
  {
   "text": "A zero on the line makes a wave about the size of root x.",
   "start": 5.888,
   "end": 9.737
  },
  {
   "text": "A zero at three quarters would make one of size x to the three quarters:",
   "start": 9.737,
   "end": 13.775
  },
  {
   "text": "far louder.",
   "start": 13.775,
   "end": 14.875
  },
  {
   "text": "So the Riemann Hypothesis says the error in counting primes stays as small as it",
   "start": 15.825,
   "end": 19.85
  },
  {
   "text": "possibly can: about root x, up to logarithms.",
   "start": 19.85,
   "end": 23.763
  },
  {
   "text": "That's the typical drift of a random walk of x coin flips.",
   "start": 23.763,
   "end": 27.438
  },
  {
   "text": "Assuming RH, the gap between pi of x and L I of x stays below root x,",
   "start": 27.438,
   "end": 32.688
  },
  {
   "text": "times log x, over eight pi.",
   "start": 32.688,
   "end": 35.413
  },
  {
   "text": "Up to a million, it does.",
   "start": 35.413,
   "end": 37.2
  }
 ],
